 { Lev Polugaevsky – Borislav Ivkov, Belgrade 1969 This game is a classical illustration of White’s ability to asphyxiate Black’s forces after a symmetric transformation. } 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. Nc3 Nc6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nxd4 6. Qxd4 g6 7. e4 d6 8. Be3 Bg7 9. f3 O-O 10. Qd2 Be6 11. Rc1 Qa5 { We have transposed from an English Opening into a popular variation of the Accelerated Dragon. } 12. Nd5 { This move has fallen out of fashion since 12...Qxa2 offers good chances to Black. } ( { Nowadays } 12. b3 { is preferred (and we will see this move in the next example) or } 12. Be2 Rfc8 13. b3 { . } ) 
Qxd2+ $6 { Heading towards an inferior endgame. } ( { Instead correct was: } 12... Qxa2 13. Nxe7 Kh8 14. Be2 Ng8 $5 ) 
13. Kxd2 Bxd5 ( 13... Nxd5 $6 { is not good due to 14. cxd5; Black is in trouble after 14...Bc8 } ( { or } 14... Bd7 { 15.Rc7± } ) 
15. b3 { e6 16.Bd3 followed by Rc7 . } ) 
 ( { A good option was } 13... Rae8 { and after the semi-forced line } 14. Bxa7 Bxd5 15. cxd5 Ra8 16. Be3 Rxa2 17. Rc2 Nd7 { White preserves a small advantage, but Black has more counterplay than he did in the game. } ) 
14. cxd5 { We have reached the structure of interest. The c-file is open and for the moment there are no natural breaks to execute. This position has occurred 27 times on my database, and White has scored an amazing 80%. } Rfc8 15. Be2 $5 { ⩲ A simple approach. White has a stable advantage due to his space and pair of bishops. His plan is to expand everywhere on the board and to eventually asphyxiate Black. The lack of targets, or natural structural breaks, prevents Black from creating counterplay. } ( { Much more common is } 15. Rxc8 Rxc8 16. g3 $1 { to follow up with Bh3 , when Black loses control of the c-file. For example: } Nd7 ( 16. Be2 a6 $2 { loses to } 17. Bh3 Rc7 ( { or } 17. Be2 Rd8 18. Rc1 ) 
18. Rc1 $1 Rxc1 19. Kxc1 { followed by Bc8 } ) 
17. Bh3 Rc7 18. Bxd7 Rxd7 19. b3 a6 20. Rc1 $16 { White gains control of the c-file. } ) 
a6 { This is necessary sooner or later, to defend the a7-pawn, but now the b6-square is weak. } ( 15... b6 $2 16. Ba6 $16 { loses the c-file. } ) 
16. b4 { Beginning the queenside expansion. } Kf8 ( { Black cannot fight for queenside space with } 16... b5 $2 { as it weakens the a6-b5 chain, and after } 17. Rxc8 Rxc8 18. Rc1 Rxc1 19. Kxc1 $16 { White is almost winning due to the threat of a2-a4. } ) 
 ( { The break } 16... e6 17. dxe6 fxe6 { weakens Black’s position, but secures some space and after } 18. a4 $6 ( { instead White should play: } 17... Kf8 17. Kd3 $1 Nd7 19. f4 $14 ) 
Kf7 19. a5 d5 $5 { Black has some counterplay. } ) 
17. a4 Nd7 18. a5 { Now the a6- and b7-pawns are fixed and represent potential weaknesses in the long term. } Bb2 19. Rc2 Rxc2+ 20. Kxc2 Bg7 21. Kb3 Rc8 22. Bd2 $1 { Black cannot use the c-file. } ( { Note how White refrains from } 22. Rc1 $6 Rxc1 23. Bxc1 { in order to keep more pieces on the board. } ) 
Bd4 23. g4 $6 { Now White will claim space on the kingside. } Kg7 $6 { Black’s waiting strategy is a big mistake. } ( { Again } 23... e6 { was correct, as after } 24. dxe6 fxe6 25. h4 $1 { ⩲ Black has many weaknesses, but at least he has space to move. } ) 
 ( { PH: Actually, White's last move was a strategic mistake, because Black could have now played } 23... g5 $1 { when } 24. Bxg5 Rc3 25. Ka4 Be3 { leads to good activity for Black, enough for equality. } ) 
24. g5 $1 { Fixing Black’s kingside. } Rc7 25. Rd1 Kf8 26. f4 Bg7 ( { It was not too late for } 26... e6 $5 27. dxe6 fxe6 28. Bc4 Ke7 { though after } 29. h4 $1 { ± the threat of h4-h5 is troublesome. } ) 
27. Bg4 Ke8 28. Rf1 Bd4 29. h4 Bg7 30. h5 Bd4 31. Rh1 $16 { Throughout the last eight moves Black’s negligent waiting strategy has allowed a powerful kingside expansion. Although there is no clear winning method yet, White’s position is very promising and should be won with correct play. } Bg7 32. Rh3 Kf8 33. h6 $1 { White has found the right plan. Now he will prepare the decisive e4-e5 break, after which Black’s main problem will be the weak h7-pawn. } Bd4 34. Rd3 ( { White is already winning after: } 34. Bc3 $1 Rxc3 ( { or } 34. Rd3 Bg1 35. e5 $1 { is similar to the game } ) 
35. Rxc3 Bxc3 36. Kxc3 Ke8 37. Kd4 Kd8 38. e5 $1 { (threatening the decisive e5-e6) } Kc7 39. e6 fxe6 40. Bxe6 Nf8 41. f5 gxf5 42. Bxf5 Kd8 43. Bxh7 $1 { When the passed pawns decide the game. } ) 
Ba7 ( { PH: Probably Polugaevsky played 34.Rd3 because he didn't want to calculate the endgame after 34.Bc3 !, and instead wanted to answer } 34... Bh8 { with } 35. e5 $5 dxe5 36. Rc3 $1 { , forcing } Rxc3 37. Bxc3 Ke8 38. Bxd7 Kxd7 39. fxe5 { and the win should be trivial since Black will soon get into zugzwang. } ) 
35. Bh3 Bg1 36. Bc3 { Preparing the e4-e5 break. } Ke8 ( { More stubborn was } 36... Bh2 { though White is still winning, say after: } 37. Rf3 Ke8 38. Bg7 Kd8 ( { or } 38... Rc1 39. e5 $1 dxe5 40. Bxd7 Kxd7 41. fxe5 ) 
39. f5 $1 { Decisively opening the f-file. The game could have finished with: } Rc1 40. fxg6 fxg6 41. Bd4 Re1 42. Bb6 $1 Nxb6 ( { or } 42... Ke8 43. Bxd7 Kxd7 44. Rf8 { to follow with Rd8# } ) 
43. axb6 Ke8 44. Rc3 Rxe4 45. Be6 $1 { Checkmate is unavoidable. } ) 
37. e5 $1 { +- } Bh2 ( 37... dxe5 38. fxe5 Bh2 { doesn’t help due to } 39. d6 $1 { breaking through the defence. } exd6 40. exd6 Rc6 41. Re3 Kd8 42. Re7 Rxd6 43. Rxf7 { and after 44.Rxh7 the game is decided. } ) 
38. exd6 exd6 39. Re3+ Kd8 40. Re4 ( { More direct was: } 40. Bxd7 Rxd7 41. Bf6 Kc7 42. Re8 Bxf4 43. Rh8 $18 ) 
Bg1 41. Bxd7 $1 ( { Black resigns, as there is no hope following: } 41. Bxd7 Rxd7 ( { Or } 41. Bxd7 Kxd7 42. Bf6 { with the deadly threat Re7, and if } Kc8 { then } 43. Re8 Kd7 44. Rd8 ) 
42. Bf6 Kc7 43. Re8 { followed by Rh8xh7 winning. Final remarks 1. Black’s opening was rather imprecise, leading directly to an unfavourable endgame. Nowadays 12...Qxa2 is the move of choice and in fact White no longer plays 12. Nd5 because of this reply. 2. The endgame resulting after move 14 was indisputably superior for White, and the defence was very difficult in practice. 3. Black’s best approach would have been to break the powerful d5-e4 chain with a timely ...e7-e6 break. This weakens the d6- and e6-pawns, but it is the lesser of two evils. Instead, by defending passively Black was signing his own death sentence. White’s kingside expansion is a winning plan and passive defence is destined to fail no matter how precise. 4. White’s expansion on the flanks was enabled by his pair of bishops and his already existing spatial edge in the centre. By having more space, White is able to gain yet more. } ) 

 1-0

