 { Jan Smeets – Sergei Tiviakov, Netherlands 2013 Learning objective: This game is a modern example of White’s advantage after a symmetric structure transformation. Notice how White inevitably gains control of the c-file. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 Nxd4 7. Qxd4 d6 8. Be3 Bg7 9. f3 Be6 10. Rc1 O-O 11. Qd2 Qa5 { So far it is the same opening as the previous example, and now: } 12. b3 a6 $2 { Black confuses his move order! } ( { The main line is } 12... Rfc8 { and one of the possible continuations is } 13. Be2 ( { of course now } 12... a6 12. Nd5 $6 Qxd2 14. Kxd2 Nxd5 15. cxd5 Bd7 { is nothing special for White } ) 
a6 14. Na4 Qxd2 15. Kxd2 Nd7 16. g4 $5 { with a complex and deeply analyzed endgame. } ) 
 { And now of course: } 13. Nd5 $1 { This is similar to the previous example except the reply ...Qxa2 is no longer possible. } Qd8 { Tiviakov probably realized his mistake by now, as keeping the queens on the board gives more practical chances to Black. } ( { If } 13... Qxd2 14. Kxd2 Bxd5 ( { or } 14... Rae8 15. Be2 Nd7 16. h4 $14 ) 
15. cxd5 $14 { we are back to the highly undesirable endgame from the previous example. } ) 
14. Bb6 Qd7 15. Be2 Bxd5 { A necessary concession. } ( { If instead } 15... Rac8 16. O-O { ⩲ and Black has nothing useful to do other than capture on d5 after all. } ) 
16. cxd5 $14 { We essentially have the previous game again, but now in the presence of queens. } Rfc8 17. O-O Qe8 ( { The break } 17... e6 $6 18. dxe6 fxe6 { works poorly as the presence of queens makes Black’s king vulnerable. } ) 
18. Be3 Nd7 19. g3 ( { The careless } 19. b4 Qd8 20. a4 $6 { is met by } a5 $1 21. bxa5 Nc5 { with counterplay for Black. } ) 
Nc5 $2 { Blocking the c-file for the moment, but this will not work in the long run as White is able to play b3-b4. } ( { Better was } 19... Rxc1 20. Rxc1 Rc8 21. Rc2 Qd8 22. Kg2 h5 23. b4 Kh7 24. Qc1 Rxc2 25. Qxc2 Bh6 26. Bf2 $1 { keeping the pair of bishops. After } a5 27. a3 axb4 28. axb4 $14 { White is certainly better, though Black remains solid. } ) 
20. Rc2 a5 21. Rfc1 b6 { The problem with Black’s 19th move is that the c-file will not stay blocked forever. Meanwhile, Black’s attempts to keep the file blocked are creating weaknesses that will persist forever. For example ...b7-b6 makes the b6-pawn and the c6-square weak. } 22. Qd1 Qf8 23. a3 $1 { ± Preparing b3-b4. } Rcb8 ( { But not: } 23... Nxb3 $2 24. Rxc8 Rxc8 25. Rxc8 Qxc8 26. Qxb3 $18 ) 
24. b4 axb4 25. axb4 Nb3 $2 { The decisive mistake; the knight will not find a way out. } ( { More stubborn was } 25... Nd7 26. Bb5 Qd8 27. Qe2 Ra7 { though White has many risk-free plans to play for a win, such as } 28. Bc6 $5 h5 29. Kg2 Kh7 30. Ra2 Rxa2 31. Qxa2 $16 { following by an a-file invasion. } ) 
 ( { Not } 25... Ra3 $2 26. bxc5 Rxe3 27. c6 { followed by c6-c7 and Ba6 winning. } ) 
26. Rb1 Ra3 27. Rc6 Na1 28. Bxb6 { White is completely winning due to his extra pawn and the awkward location of the knight on a1. The rest of the game needs no comments. } f5 29. Bd4 Rba8 30. e5 Bxe5 31. Bxe5 dxe5 32. b5 e4 33. b6 exf3 34. Bxf3 Qh6 35. b7 Qe3+ 36. Kh1 Kf7 37. bxa8=Q Rxa8 38. d6 { Final remarks 1. Black’s problems began by forgetting his opening move order, and allowing the strong 13. Nd5, which is an improved version of the previous game, as the ...Qxa2 reply is no longer available. 2. Despite his opening imprecision, Black had reasonable chances to hold the game by trading rooks on move 19. 3. Black’s biggest mistake was attempting to block the c-file with 19...Nc5, as White will gain this file anyway after the standard b3-b4. Black’s problem was that supporting his knight on c5 meant creating more weaknesses with 20...a5 and 21...b6, hence later when the c-file was finally unblocked, White gained a huge advantage. } 
 1-0

