 { Jakovljevic – Predojevic, Novi Sad 2012, started out as a Maroczy and reached the position in the diagram, which is similar to previous examples. Despite not having a pair of bishops, White has a significant spatial advantage and a very promising position. The game continued: } 20... Kf8 ( { PH: Flores Rios doesn't discuss it, but we should first look if Black can counter over the c-file. He can: } 20... Rdc8 21. Rfc1 Rxc3 22. Rxc3 Rc8 ( 22... f5 $1 { is even more precise, as } 23. Rc7 { is countered by } Rc8 ) 
23. Rxc8 Bxc8 24. Kg1 f5 25. Kf2 f4 $1 { and now when White moves to the queenside, Black's king can enter via e5: } 26. Ke1 Kg7 27. Kd2 Kf6 28. Kc3 Ke5 { . So opposing on the c-file is correct, but has to be followed up actively. } ) 
 ( { The immediate } 20... e6 21. dxe6 Bxe6 { should also be okay, with counterplay against White's queenside, exploiting the fact that White's king is very far away at the moment. } ) 
21. Rfc1 Ke8 22. Kg1 Rdc8 23. Kf2 ( { If } 23. Rxc8 $5 Bxc8 24. Kf2 Kd8 25. Ke3 Ra5 26. Kd4 { Black is worse but his position seems defensible after } Bd7 { . } ) 
Kd8 24. Ke3 b6 { Hoping to install a blockade with ...Rc5. } ( { An option was } 24... Rxc3 25. Rxc3 e5 $1 { to be followed by ...f7-f5, and after } 26. dxe6 fxe6 27. f4 Ra5 $5 { the d6- and e6-pawns are not as vulnerable, and White’s king cannot manoeuvre as easily, hence Black should be able to hold a draw with precise play. } ) 
25. Rxc8+ $1 { Trading rooks will help White here, as the bishop endgame is difficult and Black’s lack of space can eventually lead to a zugzwang. } Rxc8 $2 { Without rooks on the board, Black loses all his chances for counterplay. } ( { Better was } 25... Bxc8 26. Rc6 Rb8 27. f4 Bd7 28. Rc1 Ra8 29. g4 Ra5 30. g5 f5 $5 { when White remains better, but the most likely result is draw. } ) 
26. Rxc8+ Kxc8 27. f4 Kc7 { This careless move is the decisive mistake. } ( { If } 27... e6 28. dxe6 fxe6 29. Kd4 { the absence of rooks allows White to manoeuvre freely, giving him excellent winning chances after: } Bc6 30. h4 ( { PH: } 29... Kc7 29. g3 { followed by e5 should also be winning. For example } Kc7 31. e5 d5 32. Bd3 $16 Be8 33. g4 Bd7 34. Bc2 Kd8 35. f5 $18 gxf5 36. gxf5 exf5 37. Kxd5 { and White will soon pick up either the f5 or the a4-pawn. } ) 
Kd8 31. g4 Ke7 32. g5 $16 ) 
 ( { Necessary was } 27... f6 $1 { to prevent White from placing a pawn on g5; } 28. h4 ( { Or } 27... Kc7 27. g4 g5 $1 ) 
h6 29. g4 $6 ( { Instead } 28... Kc7 28. Kd4 Kc7 30. e5 $1 { is more promising. } ) 
g5 $1 30. fxg5 fxg5 31. hxg5 hxg5 { And now at least the g4-pawn is fixed on its own bishop’s colour. White still has a promising position after } 32. e5 $1 dxe5 33. Ke4 $14 { but Black keeps drawing chances, say after } Kd8 34. Kxe5 Ke8 35. a3 Kf7 { . } ) 
28. g4 h6 ( { Keeping the kingside intact makes no difference. For example: } 28... b5 29. Kd4 Kb6 30. a3 Ka5 31. Kc3 { The queenside is paralyzed; now White proceeds on the kingside. } Bc8 32. h4 Bd7 33. g5 Bh3 34. h5 $1 Bg2 35. hxg6 hxg6 36. Bd3 $18 { Followed by Kd4, f4-f5xg6 and we reach the same structure as in the game. } ) 
29. h4 f6 ( { If Black wants to refrain from ...f7-f6, to keep the g6-pawn defended, then a possible continuation is: } 29... b5 30. Kd4 Kb6 31. a3 Be8 32. g5 hxg5 33. hxg5 Bd7 34. f5 $1 gxf5 ( { or } 34... Be8 35. fxg6 fxg6 36. e5 $1 { is similar to the game } ) 
35. Bh5 { And White wins after Bxf7. } ) 
30. g5 $1 { Just as in Polugaevsky – Ivkov, White expands on the kingside to create weaknesses in Black’s camp. Black is helpless as he will be unable to protect the g6-pawn. } fxg5 31. fxg5 hxg5 ( { Or } 31... h5 32. Kd4 e5 ( { Or } 32... b5 33. e5 { is similar to the game. } ) 
33. dxe6 Bxe6 34. Bb5 $1 { (threatening Be8 ) } Kd8 35. a3 Bb3 36. Bc4 $1 { forcing a winning pawn endgame after } Bxc4 37. Kxc4 Ke7 38. Kb5 { . } ) 
32. hxg5 b5 33. Kd4 Kb6 ( { Keeping the king centralized does not help either: } 33... Be8 34. a3 Kb6 35. Bd3 Kc7 36. e5 dxe5 37. Kxe5 Kd8 38. Ke6 $1 { +- Black is in zugzwang. } ) 
34. Bd3 Ka5 35. a3 Kb6 ( 35... b4 { does not help: } 36. e5 Be8 37. e6 { Black is in zugzwang, thus he must take on a3: } bxa3 { } 38. bxa3 Kb6 { And now White must lose a tempo, hence he plays: } 39. Be4 ( 38... Kb6 38. Kc3 $2 Kc5 ) 
Ka5 40. Bf3 Kb5 41. Be2 Kb6 42. Bd3 $1 { Now Black is in a new zugzwang: } Ka5 43. Kc3 Kb6 44. Kb4 Kc7 45. Ka5 Kb7 46. Bb5 $1 { The resulting pawn endgame is winning. } ) 
36. e5 Be8 37. e6 { Black is completely lost, and the game finished with the nice sequence: } Ka5 38. Kc3 Ka6 39. Kb4 Kb6 40. Bc2 Ka6 41. b3 axb3 42. Bxb3 Kb6 43. Bc2 Ka6 44. Bd3 Kb6 45. Be2 ( { Zugzwang. } 45. Be2 Ka6 46. a4 Kb6 47. Bxb5 { Final remarks 1. Once again, White’s spatial advantage allows him to gain even more space. 2. Unlike previous examples, White did not have a pair of bishops here, but still managed to make problems for Black. 3. Black’s position was defensible, say with 24...Rxc3 followed by ...e5. Nevertheless, almost every line required precision from Black, while White could play for a win risk-free. 4. Something to mention from the initial position is Black’s pawn on a4, which is the same colour as his bishop. If this pawn had been on a5 to begin with then Black would have had better chances to hold the game, at least in the bishop endgame. } ) 

 1-0

