 { Now the game Navara – Jirovsky, Czech Republic 2003, continued with: } a4 $1 { Black has a trick in mind. } 3. b4 $6 { Overly optimistic, though it is hard to blame White for overlooking Black’s reply. } ( { Better was: } 22. bxa4 Rxa4 23. Bb5 ( { or } 23. Rxb7 $5 Rxa2 $1 24. Rxa2 Nxb7 25. Rc2 { when White has a tiny plus } ) 
Raa8 24. a4 Nd7 25. Bf1 Rxc2 26. Qxc2 Rc8 27. Qd2 Rc7 { White is still somewhat better, but now Black certainly can find more counterplay than in the previous examples. } ) 
Nb3 $1 { A strong recurring idea in this position – the knight is immune. } ( { PH: Even } 3... Nd7 $5 { is very reasonable, especially if Black is allowed to play ...a3 as well, breaking the connection of White's queenside pawns and making ...Bb2 possible to annoy White. So } 4. a3 Rxc2 5. Qxc2 Rc8 { and Black gets the rook to c3 with counterplay: } 6. Qxa4 Rc3 7. Bd2 Nb6 { and now White either has to return the extra pawn by playing } 8. Qd1 { or keep the queen on the a-file somewhere and allow ...Rc2. } ) 
4. axb3 ( { Black takes the initiative after } 23. Qd1 $6 Rxc2 24. Qxc2 Nd4 25. Qd3 Nxe2 26. Qxe2 Rc8 { . } ) 
 ( { An option was } 23. Rxc8 Qxc8 $1 24. Qxc8 ( { but not } 24. axb3 a3 $1 { -+ } ) 
Rxc8 { and now Black is fine after } 25. Bb5 ( { again not } 25. axb3 $2 a3 $19 ) 
Rc3 26. Bxa4 Rxe3 27. Bxb3 Bc3 $5 { with a drawish position. } ) 
a3 5. Rc4 $2 ( { Necessary was } 24. Bc4 a2 25. Ra1 Bxa1 26. Qxa1 { though after } b5 $1 { the bishop is lost: } 27. Rxa2 ( { White is losing after } 27. Bd3 $2 Rxc2 28. Bxc2 Qc8 $1 { and now for example } 29. Bd3 Ra3 30. Bg1 Rxb3 31. Bxb5 Qc2 ) 
Rxa2 28. Qxa2 bxc4 29. bxc4 Ra8 30. Qb2 Ra4 { And we obtain a dynamically balanced position where either side could play for the win. } ) 
a2 6. Ra1 Rxc4 7. Bxc4 ( { The alternative } 26. bxc4 $2 { loses after the forced line: } Qf8 27. Qd1 Bxa1 28. Qxa1 Qg7 29. Qxg7 Kxg7 30. Bd4 Kf8 31. Ba1 Ra4 ) 
