 { Nikolai Ninov – Dragoljub Velimirovic, Belgrade 2009 Learning objectives: 1. This game illustrates White’s attacking chances after an asymmetric transformation. 2. Note Black’s inability to create counterplay once the queenside is closed. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. c4 Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Nc3 d6 8. Be2 O-O 9. O-O Bd7 10. Qd2 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 Bc6 12. f3 a5 13. b3 Nd7 14. Be3 Nc5 15. Rab1 { So far we are in the main line of the Accelerated Dragon, and now: } b6 { This is a rare and low-scoring option. } ( { The main line is } 15... Qb6 { (PH: A typical continuation is } 16. Rfc1 Rfc8 17. Rc2 Qb4 { trying to provoke a3. } 18. Qc1 Qb6 19. a3 Qd8 { and now the b3-pawn is a bit soft, while } 20. b4 { is answered by } axb4 21. axb4 Na4 $5 { and Black is okay.) } ) 
16. Bd1 Ra7 17. a3 Qa8 18. Bc2 Rd8 19. Nd5 { White’s position is notably comfortable and now he invites Black to transform his position after: } Bxd5 20. exd5 $1 { We have reached the thematic structure. } ( { If } 20. cxd5 Rc8 21. Rfc1 Rac7 22. Bd1 b5 { Black seems fine as he has gained some queenside space without weakening his position. } ) 
Nd7 ( { If } 20... a4 21. b4 Nd7 ( { PH: } 21... Nb3 { looks interesting, but if White delays the capture and first plays } 22. Qd3 $1 { , Black is in trouble. } ) 
22. Bd3 Rc7 23. Rfd1 $14 { White has a stable advantage, since the break } b5 { is met by } 24. Rbc1 bxc4 25. Rxc4 Rxc4 26. Bxc4 $16 { and the d5-pawn is well protected while Black’s a4-pawn is a target. } ) 
21. f4 { This thematic move prepares f4-f5 with an attack, but it enables counterplay. } ( { Better was } 21. a4 $1 { preventing ...a5-a4 , leaving Black without queenside activity. } ) 
Qc8 $2 { Returning the favour; Black misses his opportunity to complicate the game. } ( { The break } 21... a4 $1 { was necessary to obtain counterplay, and after } 22. bxa4 ( 21... Qc8 21. b4 $6 { is met by } Rc7 23. Qe2 Rdc8 24. Bd3 b5 { when White’s centre is trembling } ) 
Rc8 23. Rb4 Rac7 24. Bd3 Nc5 { White’s extra pawn is clearly worthless. Still, there are chances for a kingside attack after } 25. f5 $5 Nxa4 26. fxg6 hxg6 27. Qf2 { . } ) 
22. a4 $1 { ± This move rules out Black’s queenside reactions ...a5-a4 and ...b6-b5, and so White obtains a fully stable queenside. White is now able to conduct his kingside attack without distractions. } ( { Instead } 22. f5 $2 { allows counterplay after } a4 $1 { . } ) 
Rb7 ( { PH: If Black plays } 22... f5 { , he is not in time with ...Nf6: } 23. g4 $1 fxg4 24. f5 $1 { and Black's position is hard to defend. } ) 
23. Rbe1 Re8 24. Rf3 ( { The immediate } 24. f5 { gives Black fewer options, and after } Ne5 25. Rf4 Bf6 ( { or } 25. Rf3 Bh6 26. Rh4 Bxe3 27. Rxe3 { followed by Reh3 } ) 
26. h4 Kg7 27. Qf2 $16 { White can follow up with g2-g4 with deadly threats. } ) 
e5 ( { Preventing f4-f5 with: } 24... f5 $5 { Can be met by: } 25. g4 $1 { Forcing the opening of lines. } fxg4 ( { Worse is } 25... Rf8 $2 26. gxf5 gxf5 27. Qg2 Kh8 28. Rg3 { when White’s attack is irresistible. } ) 
26. Rf2 { Preparing f4-f5. } Bf6 ( 26... Nf6 27. f5 $1 gxf5 28. Bxf5 Qd8 29. Rg2 Kh8 30. Bd4 $18 ) 
27. Ref1 Nc5 28. f5 $1 { Keeping the e7-pawn fixed. } ( 27... e5 27. Rg2 $2 e6 $1 ) 
g5 29. Bxg5 h5 30. h3 $1 { ± When Black is unable to keep the kingside closed. } ) 
 ( { Waiting with } 24... Qd8 { allows a strong attack after } 25. f5 $1 Ne5 26. Rg3 Bf6 27. Rf1 { . Note how Black can only wait to be defeated: } Kg7 28. Rh3 Rh8 29. Bh6 Kg8 30. Qe2 Rb8 31. g4 Rc8 32. Qe4 Rc7 33. g5 Bg7 34. Bxg7 Kxg7 35. Qh4 $1 { Black’s position is hopeless: } Kg8 36. fxg6 fxg6 37. Re3 { Bringing the rook to the f-file. } Rb7 38. Re4 Rb8 39. Ref4 Qd7 40. Qh6 { Black is tied to the defence of f8 . } Qe8 41. Be4 $1 { This will be followed by Bg2-h3-e6 – a very nice finish. } ) 
25. dxe6 Rxe6 26. f5 $1 { The attack continues. } Re8 27. Ref1 Ne5 28. Rg3 Qc6 29. f6 ( { An alternative was } 29. Bd4 d5 30. Qf4 { threatening f5-f6: } Qf6 31. Kh1 ( { but not } 31. fxg6 $2 Nf3 $1 ) 
dxc4 32. fxg6 Qxf4 33. gxh7 Kh8 34. Rxf4 $16 ) 
Bh8 30. Qd4 { Threatening Be4 or Qh4 and then Rh3. } Nd7 $2 { The final mistake. } ( { More stubborn was } 30... d5 $1 31. Qxd5 Qxd5 32. cxd5 $16 { though White has a clear extra pawn. } ) 
31. Bxg6 $1 { +- } ( { Black resigns, as the following lines offer no escape: } 31. Bxg6 hxg6 ( { or } 31. Bxg6 fxg6 32. f7 Kf8 33. Qxh8 Ke7 34. fxe8=Q ) 
32. Rxg6 $1  { And checkmate is unavoidable. For example: } Kh7 ( 32. Bxg6 fxg6 33. f7 ) 
33. Qh4 Kxg6 34. Qh6 ) 
 { Final remarks 1. The reader should note how in this game the capture 20.cxd5 would not have given much to White. It is necessary to know the pros and cons of both structures in order to choose correctly. 2. Once White stabilized the queenside with a3-a4, he had a free hand for a kingside attack. It is unlikely that Black could resist successfully. } 
 1-0

