 { The position in the diagram occurred in the game Aziz – Vazquez Igarza, Madrid 2011. White’s opening phase was rather imprecise, and at this point Black is ready to play: } 1... Bxd5 $1 2. exd5 ( { Worse is: } 16. cxd5 $6 Rc1 17. Kf2 Rac8 $15 ) 
Nd7 $1 { Black finds a good opportunity to trade off White’s pair of bishops. For the rest of the game White will be weak on the dark squares. } 3. Bxg7 ( { Unfortunately } 17. Be3 $2 { loses an exchange to } Bc3 { . } ) 
Kxg7 4. Be2 ( { Slightly preferable was: } 18. Kf2 { But now Black finds counterplay with: } a5 $1 ( { While } 18. Be2 b5 $2 19. cxb5 $16 { no longer works for Black. } ) 
19. Bd3 ( { Or } 19. a4 Nc5 20. Rb2 Ra6 $1 { followed by ...Rb6 } ) 
a4 20. b4 ( 20. Re1 axb3 21. axb3 Kf8 22. Bb1 Ra3 $15 ) 
Rc7 21. Re1 Kf8 22. f4 Rac8 { Note how the c4-pawn is weaker than Black’s e7-pawn. } 23. Rc2 b5 $1 24. Rec1 bxc4 25. Bxc4 Nb6 26. Bd3 Rxc2 27. Rxc2 Rxc2 28. Bxc2 Nxd5 29. b5 Nb6 $15 { Here only Black can play for a win. } ) 
b5 $1 { Black achieves the thematic break for this structure. } 5. Kf2 ( { The capture } 19. cxb5 $2 { is met by } Rc1 20. Rd1 ( { or } 20. Bd1 axb5 $17 ) 
Rxd1 21. Kxd1 axb5 22. Bxb5 Rxa2 $1 { ∓ when White has weaknesses everywhere, and of course } 23. Bxd7 $2 { loses to } Ra1 24. Kc2 Rxh1 { . } ) 
bxc4 6. bxc4 $2 { Now the c4-pawn will be a permanent weakness. } ( { It was necessary to play } 20. Bxc4 Nb6 21. Be2 Rc5 22. Rhd1 a5 $15 { though Black is in control. } ) 
Rab8 { Black controls the b-file and has a clear target in the c4-pawn; White has no counterplay whatsoever. } 7. Rc1 Rb4 8. Rdc2 Rcb8 9. Ke3 Nc5 $17 { Black has complete control of the dark squares and White’s light-squared bishop is actually quite useless. Black can progress slowly but surely. } 10. Rc3 Kf6 11. R3c2 Ra4 12. Rd1 Ra3+ 13. Kd2 h5 { Expansion on the kingside! } 14. h4 Ke5 15. Ke1 Na4 16. Kf2 ( 30. Rdd2 { does not help: } Rb1 31. Kf2 Ra1 32. g3 a5 33. Kg2 Kf6 34. Kf2 Nc5 35. Kg2 Na6 36. Kf2 Nb4 { Black wins as the a2-pawn is lost after all. } ) 
Rb2 17. Rxb2 Nxb2 18. Rd2 Rxa2 { Black has a clear extra pawn and won convincingly. Final remarks 1. The reader should understand that this version of the asymmetric transformation was very bad for White from the start. The queens were off the board, White was forced to trade his dark-squared bishop, and most importantly, he could not prevent Black from breaking on the queenside. 2. Once Black captured ...bxc4, White’s recapture bxc4 was like accepting the loss, as Black obtained a dominant position and White found himself without a plan. At that point there was basically nothing to do other than wait. } 
 0-1

