 { Milos Pavlovic – Manuel Leon Hoyos, USA 2012 Learning objective: This game shows how Black may obtain counterplay with the ...f7-f5 break. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 d6 7. Be2 Bg7 8. Be3 O-O 9. O-O Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Bd7 11. Qd2 Bc6 12. f3 a5 13. b3 Nd7 14. Be3 Nc5 15. Rab1 { So far everything is familiar, and now: } e6 $5 { This is an interesting option and one of the main lines in this variation. Black is setting up a future ...f7-f5 break. } ( { The popular main line is } 15... Qb6 ) 
16. Rfc1 $6 { The move Rfc1 is often utilized in this opening to support a queenside expansion and fight against Black’s queen (which is typically on b6 or b4) . But since Black is trying for a different plan with ...f7-f5, this move is not as useful here. } ( { More common and logical is } 16. Rfd1 Be5 { but notice that after } 17. Bd4 Qe7 ( { but not } 17. Rfc1 Bxd4 $6 18. Qxd4 $14 ) 
18. Bxe5 $6 dxe5 { the doubled pawns are no problem, as Black will play ...f7-f5 with good counterplay. } ) 
 ( { PH: The computer says that even using the other rook on the d-file is good: } 16. Rbd1 $5 ) 
Be5 17. Bf1 ( 17. f4 $2 { loses a pawn to } Bxc3 18. Qxc3 Nxe4 { . } ) 
Qe7 { A cautious move, preparing ...f7-f5 for when the right moment comes. } ( 17... f5 18. exf5 Rxf5 { This option seems a little premature after: } 19. f4 $1 ( { The tiny slip } 18... Qe7 18. Bd4 $2 { is losing after: } Qg5 $1 20. Be3 ( { or } 19... Qe7 19. Qxg5 $2 Bxd4 { or } 20. Rd1 Bxd4 21. Qxd4 Rxf3 ) 
Qh4 21. g3 Bxg3 $1 ) 
Bg7 20. Re1 { White is a little more comfortable due to the vulnerable d6- and e6-pawns. Nevertheless the position is roughly equal. } ) 
18. Rc2 f5 $1 { A timely reaction; this break opens lines and will allow Black to find good counterplay. } 19. exf5 Rxf5 20. Bd4 ( { Now the desirable } 20. f4 $2 { loses an exchange after } Bxc3 $1 { and now } 21. Qxc3 ( { or } 21. Rxc3 Ne4 ) 
Be4 ) 
Raf8 { Black has obtained a threatening position; note how all his pieces contribute to the attack. } 21. Ne2 $2 { A careless move, and the decisive mistake. } ( { Correct was } 21. Rd1 $1 { as } Bxh2 $2 22. Kxh2 Qh4 23. Kg1 Rh5 { no longer works due to } 24. Be2 $1 { when the f3-pawn is protected and the king escapes. } ) 
 ( { But instead, against } 21. Rd1 { Black replies: } Bf4 $5 ( { or } 21. Ne2 Rg5 ) 
22. Qe1 Rh5 23. g3 Be5 ( { or } 23. Ne2 Bxf3 $5 24. gxf4 Rxf4 { with compensation } ) 
24. f4 Bxd4 25. Rxd4 e5 { followed by ...Ne6 with counterplay. } ) 
 ( 21. Bxe5 $6 { is risky for White, as the following forcing line shows: } dxe5 22. Qe3 e4 $1 23. fxe4 Nxe4 24. Nxe4 Bxe4 25. Bd3 Bxd3 26. Qxd3 Qc5 27. Kh1 Qd6 $1 28. Qe2 ( 28. Qxd6 $4 Rf1 29. Rxf1 Rxf1 ) 
e5 29. h3 e4 $5 { The position is balanced, though a little easier for Black. } ) 
Bxh2+ $1 { -+ The game was just getting started... This blow really makes a point by showing us how effective is Black’s ...f7-f5 in the creation of kingside threats. } 22. Kxh2 Rh5+ 23. Kg1 Qh4 { White resigns as there is no satisfactory defence against the threats of 24...Rxf3 or 24...Ne4, followed by 25...Qh2#. Final remarks 1. White’s post-opening play was imprecise; it was clear that Black was planning ...f7-f5 hence 16. Rfc1 was out of place. 2. The key to Black’s 17...Qe7 is to prepare ...f7-f5 so that it is not so easy for White to play f3-f4 driving away the bishop on e5, as we see in the variation 15...f5. If White cannot play f3-f4 after the trade on f5, then Black is likely to have at least enough counterplay. } 
 0-1

