[FEN "2r2r1k/1p2p2p/p1np1qp1/8/2P1Bn2/1P3P2/P1NQ2PP/4RRK1 w - - 0 1"]

 { To complete what was mentioned about White playing f3-f4, take the position in the diagram, which occurred in Simacek – Kanovsky, Czech Republic 2010. Black played ...f7-f5 a few moves earlier, but could not develop his initiative quite so effectively, and now after: } 1. g3 $1 Nh5 ( { PH: If } 1... Nh3 2. Kg2 Ng5 { now White can take on c6, but there is nothing wrong with } 3. Bd5 $5 { either. } ) 
2. f4 $16 { White controls the board, as Black can do nothing on the kingside, while his central pawns are clear targets for White. A big difference between this position and the previous game is Black’s lack of bishops, hence his inability to create many threats. } Ng7 3. Ne3 Qf7 ( { Better was } 27... Nf5 { though after: } 28. Bxc6 bxc6 ( 28... Rxc6 { would allow } 29. Nd5 Qf7 30. g4 $1 { +- and White enters on e7 next. } ) 
29. Nxf5 gxf5 30. Re3 e5 ( { or } 30... Rfe8 31. Rfe1 Qf7 32. Re6 $16 ) 
31. Rd3 $1 Rcd8 32. fxe5 Qxe5 ( { or } 32... dxe5 33. Rxd8 Rxd8 34. Rxf5 $1 Rxd2 35. Rxf6 $16 ) 
33. Re3 $16 { Black’s pawn structure is very weak. } ) 
4. f5 $1 { +- It is White who attacks now, but of course this attack is based on the strong foundation of a superior pawn structure without weaknesses like the e7-pawn. } gxf5 ( 28... g5 { doesn’t help: } 29. Nd5 h6 30. f6 $1 exf6 31. Rxf6 $18 ) 
 { And now the best continuation was: } 5. Nxf5 ( { Instead the game continued } 29. Bxf5 Nxf5 30. Nxf5 $16 { and White won convincingly. } ) 
Nxf5 6. Rxf5 Qg8 7. Rh5 Rf7 8. Bd5 { Winning an exchange and the game. } 
 1-0

