[Black "Wang Zili vs. Zhang Pengxiang"]
[Result "0-1"]

 { Wang Zili – Zhang Pengxiang, Yongchuan 2003 Learning objective: This game illustrates Black’s ...b7-b5 break. Note that after pawns are traded on b5, the a2-pawn becomes a serious target. } 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. c4 Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Nc3 O-O 8. Be2 d6 9. Rc1 $6 ( { PH: } 9. O-O { is by far the most common move here. } ) 
Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Be6 ( { PH: } 10... Bh6 $1 { is just a great move, as the rook has to move again, and after } 11. Rc2 e5 12. Be3 Bxe3 13. fxe3 { Black has no problems. } ) 
11. Qd2 { PH: In this line this turns out to be a poor square for the queen. White should just castle instead. } Qa5 12. f3 Rfc8 13. b3 a6 14. O-O $6 { A serious opening imprecision, allowing Black to seize the initiative. } ( { The main line is } 14. Be3 { , though after } b5 15. Nd5 Qxd2 16. Kxd2 Nxd5 17. cxd5 Bd7 { Black has a satisfactory version of the symmetric transformation, since White cannot easily gain space on the queenside and both rooks are likely to come off the board. For example, after } 18. Rxc8 Rxc8 19. Rc1 Rxc1 20. Kxc1 e6 $5 { which seems fairly drawish. } ) 
 ( { PH: An important detail is that after } 14. Na4 Qxd2 15. Kxd2 { the move } Nxe4 $1 { is possible, but after } 16. Ke3 $1 { things get really complicated. Black can keep the advantage with } Bh6 $1 { as taking on e4 will lead to mate. } ) 
b5 15. cxb5 ( { An interesting resource was } 15. Be3 $5 bxc4 16. bxc4 Rab8 ( { but not } 16. cxb5 Bxc4 $2 { due to } 17. Nd5 $1 Qxd2 18. Nxe7 Kf8 19. Bxd2 Kxe7 20. Rxc4 $14 ) 
 { and now } 17. Nd5 Qxd2 18. Bxd2 Nxd5 19. cxd5 { leads to a roughly equal position. } ) 
axb5 { Now White is facing a positional problem. He no longer has a spatial advantage and in fact his a2-pawn is a backward pawn, a potential target; Black’s position is preferable. } 16. Rfd1 ( { An option was } 16. Qe3 Bd7 17. f4 b4 18. Na4 $1 Rxc1 19. Rxc1 Rc8 ( { but not } 19. Rfd1 Bxa4 $2 20. Bb6 { when the queen is trapped } ) 
20. Rxc8 Bxc8 21. e5 Nd5 22. Qd2 dxe5 23. fxe5 e6 $15 { when Black is better, though White has managed to draw attention away from the weak a2-pawn. } ) 
b4 17. Na4 Rcb8 ( { More precise was } 17... Nd7 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. Qd4 Kg8 20. Qe3 { when Black is just a little better, but the position is delicate. For example, the premature } Nc5 $6 ( { better is } 20... Rab8 $5 ) 
21. Nxc5 Rxc5 22. Rxc5 dxc5 23. Rd2 { allows White to equalize. } ) 
18. Bf1 ( { Better was } 18. Bc4 $1 { as after } Bd7 19. Qe2 Bxa4 20. bxa4 Qxa4 21. Bb3 { White finds compensation for the pawn. For example, } Qa5 22. e5 dxe5 23. Rc5 Qa6 24. Qxa6 Rxa6 25. Bxe5 Rba8 26. Rb5 { regaining the pawn. } ) 
Nd7 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 20. Qe1 $2 { This unnecessarily blunders a pawn. } ( { Better was: } 20. Bc4 Bxc4 21. Rxc4 Ne5 22. Rcc1 Rc8 $15 ) 
Nb6 $1 21. Nxb6 Qxb6+ 22. Kh1 Rxa2 { Black is a pawn up and won the game without problems. Final remarks Something that bothers me about this example (and essentially every example I found with ...b7-b5) is how White was still able to hold the position and even make a draw in some variations despite having allowed ...b7-b5-b4. The truth is, this plan is often good enough to gain a tiny advantage, but the game can become oversimplified rather easily. Nevertheless, we should remember the Maroczy structure often gives better chances to White. If White has to play precisely in order to make a draw, then we could say Black’s strategy has succeeded. } 
 0-1

