 { Davorin Kuljasevic – Boris Golubovic, Bol na Bracu 2013 Learning objective: This game shows how White can break in the centre with e4-e5 to weaken Black’s camp. } 1. Nf3 c5 2. c4 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. e4 Bg7 6. Be3 d6 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. f3 Nxd4 9. Bxd4 O-O 10. Be2 Bd7 11. O-O a5 12. b3 Bc6 13. Qd2 Nd7 14. Be3 Nc5 15. Rab1 Qb6 16. Rfc1 Rfc8 17. Rc2 Qd8 18. Bf1 { Everything is theory so far, in fact there are over two hundred games on my database with this exact position, and now Black deviates from the main line with: } Be5 ( { The main line goes } 18... h5 { . } ) 
19. g3 h5 20. Bg2 { White is preparing to play f3-f4 gaining space. } Kh7 21. Re1 Qf8 $6 ( { More cautious is } 21... b6 22. Bf2 ( { but not } 21... Qf8 21. Nd5 $6 e6 $1 23. Nc3 h4 { when Black begins to create kingside play } ) 
Bg7 23. f4 $14 { though White is a little better and may speculate with either e4-e5 or f4-f5. } ) 
 ( { PH: } 21... Qh8 { is an interesting way to create activity in such positions, though it's not so good here. For instance } 22. Nd5 Qd8 23. Bg5 { is annoying. Black should probably exchange with } Bxd5 { , but after } 23. Qxd5 e6 24. Qd2 $16 { Black has several weaknesses and it's difficult to deal with the incoming f4. } ) 
22. Nd5 $1 { Good timing, threatening 23.Nb6. } Nd7 ( { If } 22... Bxd5 { , although } 23. exd5 $6 Qg7 24. f4 Bf6 { is nothing special for White, say after } 25. f5 g5 $1 { keeping the kingside closed, } ) 
 ( { White has a pleasant choice between } 22... Bxd5 23. Qxd5 e6 24. Qd2 Bg7 25. f4 $14 ) 
 ( { or } 22... Bxd5 23. cxd5 Qd8 ( { PH: Flores Rios misses an interesting tactic here: } 23... a4 $1 24. b4 Nb3 $5 { , when } 25. axb3 axb3 26. Rxc8 Qxc8 { seems to give Black enough compensation for the piece with the threats associated with the passed b-pawn. Even more interestingly, } Rxc2 26. Qxc2 a3 27. Qa2 Bb2 28. Bc1 Qg7 { is also evaluated as equal by the computer! } ) 
24. Rec1 $14 { to follow up with Qd1 , a2-a3 and b3-b4. This is similar to the earlier example Smeets – Tiviakov. } ) 
23. f4 Bg7 24. Kh1 $6 { Too slow. } ( { Better was the direct } 24. f5 $1 { and now a possible continuation is: } e6 25. fxg6 ( { or } 25. Bf4 $5 { ± } ) 
fxg6 26. Rf1 Qe8 27. Bg5 $1 { (threatenin Ne7 or Nf6) } exd5 28. exd5 Ba4 $1 29. bxa4 Ne5 30. Qe2 Qxa4 31. Be4 $16 { When Black’s kingside is seriously weakened. } ) 
Qd8 $6 { This imprecision returns the favour. } ( { If } 24... e6 { White preserves an advantage after: } 25. Nb6 ( { or } 24... Qd8 24. Rd1 $5 exd5 26. exd5 Ba4 27. bxa4 $14 ) 
Nxb6 26. Bxb6 a4 27. b4 $14 ) 
25. Nc3 $6 { Too slow again! White preserves an advantage, but misses a stronger option. } ( { Now } 25. f5 $1 { was even more promising. For example: } Bxd5 ( 25. Nc3 Ne5 $2 26. Nb6 $18 ) 
26. exd5 Ne5 27. Be4 Qd7 28. Rf1 $16 { And from the example Simacek – Kanovsky we already know this kind of attack works well. } ) 
Nc5 26. e5 $1 { The right reaction for the position; the positional threat is exd6, leaving Black with a weak d6-pawn. } Bxg2+ 27. Kxg2 Ra6 ( { A better defence was } 27... Na6 { since after } 28. exd6 Nb4 29. Rcc1 Qxd6 $1 30. Qxd6 exd6 { Black has just enough time to get rid of his weak d-pawn after } 31. Re2 Bxc3 32. Rxc3 a4 33. Rd2 axb3 34. axb3 d5 { with level chances. } ) 
 ( { But of course after } 27... Na6 { White has the strong } 28. Nd5 $1 { (threatening Nb6) which secures an advantage. For example: } Rc6 29. Qf2 Nb4 ( { or } 29... dxe5 30. fxe5 Qe8 31. Bg5 $16 ) 
30. Rd2 Nxd5 31. cxd5 Rcc8 32. Bd4 $16 ) 
 ( { If } 27... Ne6 28. exd6 Qxd6 29. Qxd6 exd6 30. Rd1 $16 { the d6-pawn is a serious weakness. } ) 
 ( 27... dxe5 { would be desirable positionally, but it just loses a piece after } 28. Qxd8 Rxd8 29. Bxc5 { . } ) 
28. Bxc5 Rxc5 ( 28... dxc5 { is met by the simple } 29. Rd1 $1 Qxd2 30. Rcxd2 f6 31. Rd7 { and White wins. } ) 
29. Ne4 Rc8 30. Ng5+ Kg8 31. Qd5 { Winning a pawn and the game. } Qe8 32. Qxb7 Qc6+ 33. Qxc6 Raxc6 34. exd6 exd6 35. Rce2 { White has a winning position and the rest is irrelevant to us. Black resigned on move 56. Final remarks 1. White’s plan of f3-f4 and the resulting position proved to be very promising, as both e4-e5 and f4-f5 were potentially strong breaks. White unfortunately missed two great opportunities to play a strong f4-f5 break. 2. Black’s problem in this game was his lack of precision in critical moments. It would have helped to play ...e7-e6 to prevent White’s knight from coming to d5, where it created threats and especially allowed the f4-f5 break. } 
 1-0

